    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The thermal stress part makes sense given our heat levels, but could you go into more detail on how the bitumen viscosity specifically interacts with the subgrade? I'm trying to figure out if the cracking is primarily a surface binder issue or if we're seeing structural failure from the bottom up due to compaction voids.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The interaction between bitumen viscosity changes and subgrade compaction can be synergistic. When the bitumen is at its highest viscosity, during cool temperatures, it can actually \"lock\" into the subgrade, binding it more tightly. However, this locked-in subgrade can also trap compaction voids, reducing the allowable strains that the pavement can tolerate. When the asphalt is suddenly heated and the viscosity decreases, this locked-in subgrade is disrupted, allowing the sebse of compaction voids to cause structural failures, manifest as cracking, particularly transverse and longitudinal cracks.",
      "source": "target",
